; status: works — axiom 6 (Justin ⑥): fold a point onto a line with the crease
; pinned through a second, fixed point. Scaffolded so the crease is a genuine
; slanted line, not a trivial diagonal/midline.
;
; Two midlines give the sheet centre .ctr. We then fold .ctr down onto the
; bottom edge with the crease forced through the corner .b. The circle centred
; at .b through .ctr meets the bottom edge in two places, so `toward .a` picks
; the landing nearer .a — an irrational (constructible) point at (1 − 1/√2, 0),
; which is why the crease is a slanted line through .b rather than an axis-
; aligned one. Render the crease pattern to see the slant; render folded
; (`fold2svg --folded`) to watch the centre swing onto the bottom edge.
paper square
mark --vmid = map .a onto .b              ; x = 1/2
mark --hmid = map .a onto .d              ; y = 1/2
.ctr = --vmid * --hmid               ; sheet centre, (1/2, 1/2)
; fold the centre onto the bottom edge, crease through corner .b
fold map .ctr onto --ab through .b toward .a

; the landing point (1 − 1/√2, 0) is irrational, and bare --ab (the builtin
; edge) has no material mark so it can't be resolved by this v1 grammar
; either. `faces` is the evaluator's crease-pattern state: the one fold
; subdivides to 2; --vmid/--hmid stay unfolded marks there (they graduate to
; F creases, adding more faces, only in the emitted FOLD)
; assert faces = 2
; assert steps = 1
; assert .ctr paper = (1/2, 1/2)
